      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points",
      "problem": "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,1,3)$ and $Q(3,-4,-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(0,1,3)$ and $Q(3,-4,-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,-2⟩-⟨0,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
