      "knowledge_point_id": "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points",
      "problem": "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(4,5,3)$ and $B(-1,2,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(4,5,3)$ and $B(-1,2,0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨4,5,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
