    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-126995",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line",
      "problem": "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(2,3,6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,-2,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "${A}_{1}(-1,1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$",
