    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-126991",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line",
      "problem": "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-1,0,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩)-⟨3,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{5},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-t)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+0+4{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,1⟩+⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨2,1,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,1⟩-⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨4,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1).$",
      "answer_fields": [
