    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-162191",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment",
      "problem": "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(4,3,5)$ and $Q(6,3,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2t+4)\\,i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,i+3\\,j+k)-(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)+t(2\\,i-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)+t(2\\,i-4\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t+4)\\,i+3\\,j+(5-4t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(5-4t)+1=9-4t.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
