      "math_academy_id": "q-162189",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment",
      "problem": "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,-1,1)$ and $Q(2,-3,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j)-(i-j+k) \\\\  & =i-2\\,j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =(t+1)\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+(1-t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t+1)+(1-t)+0=2.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
